[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings president  biden is calling to expand koba testing  with the emerging omicron variant  spreading globally the cdc says more  than 660 million covet tests have been  performed in the us during the pandemic  now costs are supposed to be covered  under most circumstances but as part of  our medical price roulette series in  collaboration with clear health costs  our consumer investigative correspondent  anna werner has been hearing from some  people who have been getting very large  and confusing bills anna what's going on  here  well nate good morning you know covet  tests are supposed to be covered if you  have insurance but we've been hearing  from people who've gotten bills for  hundreds or even thousands of dollars  like one texas resident who says he's  caught between a medical provider and  his insurance company  jaden yannick lost his 75 year old  grandmother to covet 19 in november  2020. she tested positive on a wednesday  and by the next week she  she was gone so last december when he  learned he may have been exposed he went  to get a test right away i wanted to be  really proactive and get a rapid test to  ensure that i didn't have the virus and  i wasn't going to be spreading that to  my family he went to the tulsa er and  hospital for a test he says he thought  was free but several months later i  received a bill for  seven hundred dollars or so how did you  feel  yeah i felt very angry  i felt deceived that bill for the  emergency room and lab fees then the  doctor's bill landed some thirteen  hundred dollars the total over four  thousand dollars but his insurer blue  cross blue shield of texas said not to  worry my insurance informed me that they  would be sending a check in the mail and  then i would just need to  send that check to the health care  provider he did receive a check which he  sent to the hospital but unbeknownst to  him he says a second check blue cross  sent him never arrived leaving him  fighting the hospital's bill and getting  collection calls for nearly a year  when we inquired tulsa er and hospital  told us they'll accept whatever money  his insurance company sends him and once  they get it yannick won't owe anything  more what if this happens to someone  else and they do truly believe that they  are personally liable for these charges  how are they going to be able to make  ends meet given where the economy is and  he's not the only one confused i can  tell you the consensus of our friends  that have heard about this  is what do you mean you got charged for  a cova test barry and jamie costanzo of  myrtle beach south carolina planned to  visit their grandchildren in september  but say they developed symptoms we  wanted to be sure that we weren't  contagious  and had cogn they went to the only  location they say had rapid tests  available that day the conway medical  center their results negative but they  did get the bills totaling some 570  dollars after their insurance paid they  tell you you're negative then they ask  you why you're here and then they look  in your ears nose and mouth and the next  thing you know you get a bill  non-covert relief  conway medical center told us the  costanzos went to the wrong place to the  hospital's emergency department triage  tent that if they wanted a free covid  test they should have gone to the free  drive through testing since they were  technically at the emergency room conway  says it was legally bound to medically  evaluate treat and discharge them in  this case for allergies treatment the  costanzos say they didn't ask for why  would i go to a hospital or to any  testing site for covid if i was there  for allergies can you explain  why is this so confusing part of it is  because our health care system is very  complicated and confusing professor  sabrina corlett of georgetown university  says examples like those are cropping up  even though a law passed last year  requires insurance companies to cover  tests and any associated treatments if  you get a physical exam or they say  let's test you for flu or other things  as well all that visit is supposed to be  covered by the insurer now does that  mean covered completely 100  well it's supposed to be and we have  heard of some providers tacking on  certain fees sometimes they're called  facility fees or emergency room fees and  it turns out that's exactly what we  found happened to the costanzos they  were each charged 771 dollars for an  emergency room fee which their insurer  would not pay in full leaving them  jointly with bills of about 570 dollars  their lesson for others if at all  possible don't go to the hospital  go to the pharmacy  well the costanzos are appealing their  bill they gave permission for the  medical center to discuss their care  with us their insurance company blue  choice health plan of south carolina did  not respond to our repeated requests for  comment as for jaden yannick his  insurance company says they're going to  issue that second check he needs to get  him out of this but in the meantime  tulsa er and hospitals tells us they've  since changed their procedures so  instead of the check going to the  patient who then has to send it to the  hospital now the checks will go directly  from blue cross blue shield over to the  hospital they also say they're following  all laws and regulations here as well  now if you have a medical cost story to  share with us that you want us to hear  about email us at healthcosts  cbsnews.com vlad  and werner with that critical reporting  as always anna thank you very much

